s good but the solenoid driver board transistor is shorted and will need to be replaced. The same post warns that if the solenoid driver board (SDB) transistor is repaired, and the game is powered on with a dead-shorted coil, this will blow the SDB's same transistor again, so check the coil first.\n\n- **Logic-probe and meter checks:** A homepinballrepair.com WPC guide suggests briefly grounding the transistor tab to see whether the coil fires. If the coil fires but the transistor still shows voltage across it, the transistor is shorted. That guide also notes If you have a shorted TIP, you likely have fried the pre-driver transistor 2N5401.\n\n- **Power driver board capacitors:** A Pinside forum thread on a WPC game recommends replacing the capacitors C4 and C2 on the power driver board, since C4 is well known for +5v problems. This is a separate suspect from the transistor, but it's worth checking on an older board.\n\n- **Power-up locking on:** A Pinball Rehab article describes a jet bumper that locks on every time the game powers up. It warns that if the game is not turned off immediately this problem can at best blow the solenoid fuse or at worst fry the coil or circuitry on the power supply board. Turn the game off right away if a coil locks on.\n\nI didn't find a page on PinWiki that matches your exact symptom, so these are the closest matches. The most common causes are a shorted driver transistor, a missing diode tieback, or a bad pre-driver transistor. Check each coil's resistance before powering the game on again."],"durationSeconds":6.064062899999961,"searchCount":1},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ba79a434-fa96-4866-9e1b-f80179e2f271","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/c/sources/the-shadow-pinball-troubleshoot","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":null,"remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]},"error":"not_a_repo"},"live_cwd":"/c/sources/the-shadow-pinball-troubleshoot","platform":"windows"}