{"parentUuid":"d8d29620-a026-495c-89af-29ed7aba0294","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"998498a1-a27e-413b-add6-ea4b12d88e02","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UGBkHL7H99C3FpigmrK1jM","type":"tool_result","content":"The page doesn't contain the detailed A-12697 content you asked for. It doesn't describe the solenoid driver topology by group (sol 1-8, 9-16, flashers, general purpose), the latch, the diode tieback, fuses F101-F116, blanking, or a locked-coil procedure. It also doesn't mention TIP36C, TIP107, 2N5401, or 2N4401. It is a bench-test and repair guide for WPC and WPC-95 driver boards, and its troubleshooting is organized by output connector (J-numbers), not by solenoid group.\n\nHere is the related material the page does provide:\n\n- **Board types:** It lists A12697-1, A12697-3, and A12697-4 for WPC, and 20028 for WPC-95. The main difference it notes is that some WPC boards lack a flipper enable relay, which sits at the top left of the board.\n- **Test points:** For WPC, it gives expected voltages at TP1 (12 V), TP2 (5 V), TP3 (12 V), TP6 (50 V), TP7 (20 V), and TP8 (18 V).\n- **Output groups and transistors:** Each connector section names the drivers and output transistors to follow. For example, the J130 group (\"High current solenoids\") runs from U5 through pre-drivers Q71-Q74 and Q59-Q62, drivers Q63-Q69 (odd numbers), and output transistors Q64-Q70 and Q76-Q82 (even numbers). The page's listings for some groups don't match cleanly, so check them against the schematic.\n- **Failure patterns:** For general purpose outputs, it says that when an output is missing, \"Mostly it will be the power outputs ( TIP102) that will go bad..\" It also notes that if the flipper enable relay never clicks, you should \"replace Q99.\"\n- **General illumination (J121, J106):** The page attributes stuck-on lamps to the TRIAC, with the drivers listed as Q17, Q9, Q13, Q15, Q11 and the TRIACs as Q18, Q10, Q14, Q16, Q12 for J121. For J106 it lists drivers Q6-Q8 and TRIACs Q13, Q4, Q5. It also names diodes D25, D26, D29, and D30 for the unswitched outputs.\n- **Lamp columns (J138, J123):** These need a voltmeter rather than the LED strip, because the polarity is reversed. The page names U9, U18, U19, and Q91-Q98 for J138, and U10, U11, and Q93-Q100 for J123.\n- **Test procedure:** A signal generator box drives the board through J113 (J102 on some boards), with a 5 V feed from a test point. Connect only J101 and J103 (or J127-J129 on WPC-95) first to check the power supplies, and confirm the control LEDs blink before testing outputs.\n\nFor the A-12697 solenoid driver details, you'll need the original schematic (the page links to \"WPC_power_drive_board.pdf\") or a dedicated A-12697 service document."}]},"uuid":"73ad6272-50e4-45b3-b0a1-fdbb5e611579","timestamp":"2026-10-25T10:12:04.946Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":44122,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The page doesn't contain the detailed A-12697 content you asked for. It doesn't describe the solenoid driver topology by group (sol 1-8, 9-16, flashers, general purpose), the latch, the diode tieback, fuses F101-F116, blanking, or a locked-coil procedure. It also doesn't mention TIP36C, TIP107, 2N5401, or 2N4401. It is a bench-test and repair guide for WPC and WPC-95 driver boards, and its troubleshooting is organized by output connector (J-numbers), not by solenoid group.\n\nHere is the related material the page does provide:\n\n- **Board types:** It lists A12697-1, A12697-3, and A12697-4 for WPC, and 20028 for WPC-95. The main difference it notes is that some WPC boards lack a flipper enable relay, which sits at the top left of the board.\n- **Test points:** For WPC, it gives expected voltages at TP1 (12 V), TP2 (5 V), TP3 (12 V), TP6 (50 V), TP7 (20 V), and TP8 (18 V).\n- **Output groups and transistors:** Each connector section names the drivers and output transistors to follow. For example, the J130 group (\"High current solenoids\") runs from U5 