{"parentUuid":"e9ae34bb-5d4a-47c4-a457-3a3de61bfe74","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"998498a1-a27e-413b-add6-ea4b12d88e02","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CXratSSFwaP2Fmwsm2LLSV","type":"tool_result","content":"**Page 32: WilliamsMidway WPC 91 Schematics (16-9289)**\n\n**Connector and component designators**\n- Format: the first digit is the board number and the second is the position on that board. J101 is board 1, jack 1; J306 is board 3, jack 6.\n- Pin numbers follow a hyphen: J103-5 is board 1, jack 3, pin 5.\n- Fuses use the same scheme: F501 is board 5, fuse 1.\n\n**Board prefixes**\n- 1: Power Driver Board\n- 2: CPU Board\n- 3: Display Driver Board\n- 4: Dual or Single Display Board\n- 5: Sound Board\n- 6: Dot Matrix Controller Board\n- 7: Printer Kit Boards\n\n**Circuit boards**\n- **CPU Board**, p/n A-12742-x: has a 1M or 2M Game ROM, a Custom Chip, and a 68B09E microprocessor. It controls all logic and switch functions.\n- **Power Driver Board**, p/n A-12697: not game-specific, so it can move between games unchanged. It handles lamp, general illumination, flipper, and solenoid circuits. Outputs: +18V (lamps), +50V (solenoids), +5V (logic), +12V (switches), and 6.3V (general illumination).\n- **Display Driver Board**, p/n A-12739-1, -2, or -3: the hyphen number is the count of extended displays (none, one, or two, respectively). It drives the display glasses and is not game-specific when the correct suffix is used.\n- **Single Display Board**, p/n A-12794: holds one 16-digit alphanumeric display glass and is not game-specific.\n\n**Access**\nCircuit boards are in the backbox. Access them by unlocking the backbox, removing the backglass, and unlatching and swinging open the insert board. On Williams games, the speaker panel must be removed first: lift it up and forward out of the backbox and lay it on the playfield glass.\n\n**Links**\n- View Full Version: `../../../index.html#/32/zoomed`\n- Table of Contents: `./toc.html`\n- Previous page (Page 31): `./page31.html`\n- Next page (Page 33): `./page33.html`\n- Page images: `./page32_images/0001.jpg`, `./page32_images/0002.jpg`\n- Navigation icons: `./style/prevactive.png`, `./style/nextactive.png`\n- Publisher credit: `http://page-flip.com/products/publisher/`\n\nI've summarized the page rather than reproducing it verbatim in full. The source text also has OCR artifacts (for example, \"ident ified\" and \"ilJumi nation\"), so verify part numbers against the original schematic before relying on them."}]},"uuid":"95611dfb-66a7-4b6f-a429-26609c74dd26","timestamp":"2026-10-25T10:12:30.049Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":18989,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Page 32: WilliamsMidway WPC 91 Schematics (16-9289)**\n\n**Connector and component designators**\n- Format: the first digit is the board number and the second is the position on that board. J101 is board 1, jack 1; J306 is board 3, jack 6.\n- Pin numbers follow a hyphen: J103-5 is board 1, jack 3, pin 5.\n- Fuses use the same scheme: F501 is board 5, fuse 1.\n\n**Board prefixes**\n- 1: Power Driver Board\n- 2: CPU Board\n- 3: Display Driver Board\n- 4: Dual or Single Display Board\n- 5: Sound Board\n- 6: Dot Matrix Controller Board\n- 7: Printer Kit Boards\n\n**Circuit boards**\n- **CPU Board**, p/n A-12742-x: has a 1M or 2M Game ROM, a Custom Chip, and a 68B09E microprocessor. It controls all logic and switch functions.\n- **Power Driver Board**, p/n A-12697: not game-specific, so it can move between games unchanged. It handles lamp, general illumination, flipper, and solenoid circuits. Outputs: +18V (lamps), +50V (solenoids), +5V (logic), +12V (switches), and 6.3V (general illumination).\n- **Display Driver Board**, p/n A-12739-1, -2, or -3: the hyphen number is the count of extended displays (none, one, or two, respectively). It drives the display glasses and is not game-specific when the correct suffix is used.\n- **Single Display Board**, p/n A-12794: holds one 16-digit alphanumeric display glass and is not game-specific.\n\n**Access**\nCircuit boards are in the backbox. Access them by unlocking the backbox, removing the backglass, and unlatching and swinging open the insert board. On Williams games, the speaker panel must be removed first: lift it up and forward out of the backbox and lay it on the playfield glass.\n\n**Links**\n- View Full Version: `../../../index.html#/32/zoomed`\n- Table of Contents: `./toc.html`\n- Previous page (Page 31): `./page31.html`\n- Next page (Page 33): `./page33.html`\n- Page images: `./page32_images/0001.jpg`, `./page32_images/0002.jpg`\n- Navigation icons: `./style/prevactive.png`, `./style/nextactive.png`\n- Publisher credit: `http://page-flip.com/products/publisher/`\n\nI've summarized the page rather than reproducing it verbatim in full. The source text also has OCR artifacts (for example, \"ident ified\" and \"ilJumi nation\"), so verify part numbers against the original schematic before relying on them.","durationMs":7386,"url":"https://planetarypinball.com/reference/partsmanuals/WMS_Schematic_WPC_16-9289/files/assets/seo/page32.html"},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"0004d454-c0dd-484c-b4da-b2d907b1b019","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/c/sources/the-shadow-pinball-troubleshoot","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":null,"remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]},"error":"not_a_repo"},"live_cwd":"/c/sources/the-shadow-pinball-troubleshoot","platform":"windows"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e9ae34bb-5d4a-47c4-a457-3a3de61bfe74","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"67a0b140-c89f-4ca5-be8f-b924fa2c867f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\sources\\the-shadow-pinball-troubleshoot","sessionId":"67a0b140-c89f-4ca5-be8f-b924fa2c867f","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
